{"ts": "2026-08-17T11:55:56Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 102, "kind": "tool_use", "text": "Write", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "tool_use", "tool_use_id": "toolu_019J2Rw3FwafYNcouyFG7zLL", "input": {"file_path": "/home/dev/projects/test-project/docs/spec.md", "content": "# Spec: Clear all completed tasks at once\n\nTaiga #9\n\n## Workspace check\n\n`git status` is clean, `git log` shows the branch tip (`9c6eea4`) is the\nalready-merged \"Keep tasks when the server restarts\" (Taiga #5) work. No\nforeign in-progress work in the tree. `docs/spec.md` and `docs/design.md`\nwere leftover from that finished Taiga #5 cycle; both are overwritten by this\nspec since that story is already merged and there is nothing left to lose.\n\n## Triage\n\n**Feature** \u2014 net-new capability. Touches three layers (store, route,\nfrontend), but each layer is a thin, additive slice of an established\npattern already used by every other mutation (`complete_task`,\n`delete_task`, etc.) \u2014 there's no new architectural concept to introduce.\nKeeping this as one spec rather than splitting: the \"split across layers\"\nrule exists to stop one cycle from carrying disproportionate weight, and\nhere the backend half is ~15 lines across two files and the frontend half is\none new ~20-line component plus two call sites. Splitting would produce two\nreviews of trivial, mutually-meaningless partial features.\n\n## Goal\n\nLet me remove every completed task in one action, instead of deleting them\none at a time.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. **Given** zero completed tasks exist, **when** I look at the clear-completed\n   control, **then** it is disabled and shows a count of 0; clicking it (if\n   somehow triggered) makes no API call and removes nothing.\n2. **Given** N completed tasks and M open tasks (N > 0), **when** I trigger\n   clear-completed, **then** exactly N tasks are removed, all M open tasks\n   are unchanged (same id, title, priority, done=false), and the control's\n   count reflects N before the action runs.\n3. **Given** clear-completed has just run, **when** I `GET /api/tasks`,\n   **then** no task in the response has `done: true`.\n4. **Given** the current list filter is \"open\" or \"done\" (not \"all\"),\n   **when** I trigger clear-completed, **then** it still removes every\n   completed task store-wide \u2014 the action is not scoped to what's currently\n   visible under the filter.\n5. **Given** clear-completed has just run, **when** the server restarts\n   (store reloads from `TASKS_DATA_FILE`), **then** the removed tasks do not\n   reappear \u2014 the on-disk snapshot reflects the removal, same guarantee\n   every other mutation already gives (Taiga #5).\n6. **Given** the task list is entirely empty (no tasks at all, not just no\n   completed ones), **when** `DELETE /api/tasks/completed` is called\n   directly, **then** it returns `200` with a count of `0`, not an error.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- No undo. This mirrors the existing per-task Delete button, which is also\n  irreversible.\n- No confirmation modal / `window.confirm` dialog. Nothing else in this app\n  interrupts a destructive action with a popup (per-task Delete doesn't\n  either); \"tells me how many it will remove\" is satisfied by the count\n  being visible on the control before it's clicked, not by a popup.\n- No selective clearing (by priority, by age, by title match) \u2014 \"every\n  completed task,\" full stop.\n- No keyboard shortcut.\n- No change to how individual tasks are completed or deleted.\n\n## Affected areas\n\n- `backend/app/store.py` \u2014 add `TaskStore.clear_completed() -> int`,\n  following the exact shape of `delete()`/`set_done()`: mutate `self._tasks`,\n  call `self._save()` once if anything changed, return the number removed.\n- `backend/app/models.py` \u2014 add a response model (no bare dicts, per\n  convention), e.g. `ClearResult(BaseModel)` with a single `count: int`\n  field.\n- `backend/app/main.py` \u2014 add `DELETE /api/tasks/completed`, response_model\n  `ClearResult`, calling `store.clear_completed()`.\n  **Ordering hazard, confirmed while reading this file**: the existing\n  `@app.delete(\"/api/tasks/{task_id}\")` at line 56 has no `:int` converter\n  in the path string, so Starlette matches `/api/tasks/completed` against it\n  as a plain string first (converter mismatch happens at\n  FastAPI's pydantic-validation stage, not at route-matching), producing a\n  `422` for `task_id=\"completed\"` instead of ever reaching a route\n  registered later. The new route **must** be declared above line 56, not\n  below it.\n- `backend/tests/test_tasks.py` \u2014 new tests alongside the existing\n  `test_<behavior>` style (see e.g. lines 34\u201341 for the delete-single\n  pattern).\n- `frontend/src/api.js` \u2014 add `clearCompleted = () => request('/api/tasks/completed', { method: 'DELETE' })`, same one-liner shape as the existing exports.\n- `frontend/src/components/` \u2014 one new component file for the control\n  (one component per file, per project convention). Exact placement/copy is\n  the ux-designer's call; functionally it needs the completed count and an\n  onClear handler.\n- `frontend/src/App.jsx` \u2014 compute `completedCount = tasks.filter(t =>\n  t.done).length` (note: from the full `tasks` list, not `visibleTasks` \u2014\n  ties to AC4, the control's count must not be filter-scoped), render the\n  new control, wire its handler through the existing `run()` helper\n  (line 37) so errors surface through the existing `error` banner\n  (line 52) the same way every other action already does.\n- `frontend/src/App.css` \u2014 styling for the new control, matching existing\n  button conventions (lines 16\u201323).\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- Zero completed tasks: control is inert (AC1). No API call fires.\n- All tasks are completed: list becomes empty afterward, same empty-state\n  copy path already used elsewhere (`TaskList`'s `EMPTY_COPY`).\n- Mixed completed/open: only completed removed, open tasks' ids/order\n  untouched (AC2).\n- Action triggered while a filter other than \"all\" is active: still\n  store-wide (AC4).\n- Backend restart after clearing: removal persists (AC5).\n- Direct API call against an already-empty store: `200`/count `0`, not a\n  404 or error (AC6) \u2014 there's no per-item id here to 404 against, unlike\n  the single-delete route.\n- Network/API failure mid-clear: surfaces through the existing `error` state\n  in `App.jsx`, same as every other mutation \u2014 no new error-handling path\n  needed.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **How the count is communicated** (\"tells me how many it will remove\"):\n  proceeding under the assumption that an inline, always-visible count on\n  the control (e.g. a label showing the number) satisfies this, rather than\n  a confirmation dialog \u2014 justified above under Non-goals, since no other\n  destructive action in this app uses a dialog. If the actual product intent\n  was a pre-delete confirmation step, that's a different, larger UI pattern\n  this app doesn't have anywhere yet and would need sign-off before the\n  ux-designer builds it \u2014 override here if that reading is wrong.\n- **Response shape for the bulk-clear endpoint**: proceeding under a new\n  `ClearResult{count: int}` model (200 OK) rather than reusing `Task`'s\n  204-no-content convention from single delete, because this route has no\n  single task id to report \u2014 flagging in case a different contract (e.g.\n  returning the list of removed task ids) is wanted for some downstream\n  consumer; nothing in this codebase currently needs that.\n"}, "session_id": "b8a9613a-1db8-444f-996f-d0fba8901ef0"}}
